two suits, so One Voided Suit (1 point).  \n- All Simples? All my tiles are simples (2-8 of suited tiles): T1 is terminal (1), so not all simples if I keep T1. T9 is terminal. So if I discard terminals, I can aim for All Simples (2 points).  \n\nSo to maximize points, I should aim for hand with at least 8 points. Currently very low: maybe 1-2 points. Need more high-value patterns.  \n\nGiven the situation, I should think about discarding a tile that improves flexibility and potential for higher points.  \n\nEvaluation of discard options:  \n- Play W4: Removes isolated Character, leads to One Voided Suit (if I later discard T1/T9 as well). But W4 could be used for chow with W2,W3 or W4,W5,W6, but those are less likely.  \n- Play T1: Removes terminal, but breaks T1,T2,T3 chow possibility. That chow is ready now. Discarding T1 loses a ready meld.  \n- Play T6: Isolated tile, but could combine with T4,T5 or T5,T7 or T7,T8. Not great.  \n- Play B6 or B7: These are part of potential second chow with B5 or B8. Discarding them loses that chance.  \n- Play T8 or T9: These are part of potential chow with T7 or pung. T8,T9 are terminals? T8,T9 are 8 and 9, so terminals? In Mahjong, terminals are 1 and 9. So T9 is terminal, T8 is not. Discarding T9 reduces terminals, but breaks pair possibility.  \n- Play T2 or T3: Breaks T1,T2,T3 chow.  \n\nBest to keep flexible combinations. The T1,T2,T3 chow is a solid meld in waiting. So keep T1,T2,T3 together.  \nThe T8,T9 group: with two T8 and two T9, I have two pairs currently. In standard hand, only one pair allowed, so I need to convert one into a meld. Best is to aim for T7 to make chow T7,T8,T9 (using one T8 and one T9). Then leftover T8 and T9 become a pair (choose either T8 or T9 as pair). That is promising.  \n\nB6,B7: can aim for B5 or B8 to complete chow, or get another B6/B7 for pung.  \n\nGiven remaining tiles:  \n- B5: 1 left  \n- B8: 1 left  \n- T7: 4 left (good chance)  \n- T8: 4 left  \n- T9: 4 left  \n\nSo drawing T7 is relatively high probability.  \n\nThus, I should discard a tile that doesn't break potential and maybe improves tile efficiency.  \n\nW4 is least useful, as it's isolated and of a third suit. Discarding W4 opens One Voided Suit possibility and removes a stray tile.  \n\nAlso, by discarding W4, I can aim for Half Flush? No, because I still have two suits (Bamboo and Dots), but Half Flush requires one suit plus honors. No honors, so not Half Flush.  \n\nBut keeping W4 might allow Mixed Straight? Unlikely.  \n\nThus, discard W4 seems reasonable.  \n\nReason:  \nW4 is a lone tile in the Character suit, which is not prominent in my hand. Discarding it reduces the number of suits to two (Bamboo and Dots), which could yield the \"One Voided Suit\" pattern (1 point) if I eventually form a winning hand without Characters. More importantly, it preserves my strong Dots sequences (T1,T2,T3 chow and T8,T9 potential chow/pung) and Bamboo connections (B6,B7). This keeps the hand flexible for drawing T7 or B5/B8 to complete melds, moving toward a ready hand with at least some scoring patterns like All Chows, No Honors, One Voided Suit, possibly Pure Double Chow if I get another B6,B7,B8 chow.\n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
